                "target_scores": {
                    "The mass spectrometry data shows:\n- Base peak (100% intensity) at m/z 180.04225873612\n- Isotope peaks at m/z 181, 182, 183, etc. with decreasing intensities\n\nThe base peak at m/z 180.042 likely represents the molecular ion [M+H]+ or [M-H]- depending on the ionization mode in ESI. Given the context and typical ESI behavior, this is most likely [M+H]+, meaning the molecular weight is approximately 179.042.\n\nLet me analyze what molecular formula could give this mass:\n- The exact mass is 180.042 for the ion\n- If this is [M+H]+, the neutral molecule mass is ~179.042\n\nLooking at common organic molecules with this mass:\n- C6H9NO5 = 6(12.000) + 9(1.008) + 1(14.003) + 5(15.995) = 72 + 9.072 + 14.003 + 79.975 = 175.05 (too low)\n- C9H9NO3 = 9(12.000) + 9(1.008) + 1(14.003) + 3(15.995) = 108 + 9.072 + 14.003 + 47.985 = 179.06 (close!)\n- C10H9O3 = 10(12.000) + 9(1.008) + 3(15.995) = 120 + 9.072 + 47.985 = 177.057 (too low)\n- C6H9NO6 = 6(12.000) + 9(1.008) + 1(14.003) + 6(15.995) = 72 + 9.072 + 14.003 + 95.97 = 191.045 (too high)\n\nLet me recalculate more precisely:\nFor m/z 180.042 as [M+H]+:\nNeutral mass = 179.042\n\nUsing exact masses:\n- C: 12.000\n- H: 1.00783\n- N: 14.00307\n- O: 15.99491\n\nC9H9NO3:\n9(12.000) + 9(1.00783) + 1(14.00307) + 3(15.99491) = 108 + 9.07047 + 14.00307 + 47.98473 = 179.05827 (close but slightly high)\n\nC6H9NO5:\n6(12.000) + 9(1.00783) + 1(14.00307) + 5(15.99491) = 72 + 9.07047 + 14.00307 + 79.97455 = 175.04809 (too low)\n\nLet me try C10H9NO2:\n10(12.000) + 9(1.00783) + 1(14.00307) + 2(15.99491) = 120 + 9.07047 + 14.00307 + 31.98982 = 175.06336 (too low)\n\nC9H9NO3 seems closest. Let me verify: 179.058 + 1.008 (for H+) = 180.066, which is slightly higher than 180.042.\n\nLet me try C6H9NO5 again more carefully:\n6(12.000) + 9(1.00783) + 14.00307 + 5(15.99491) = 72 + 9.07047 + 14.00307 + 79.97455 = 175.04809\nAdding H+: 176.056 (too low)\n\nActually, for exact mass 180.042 (as [M+H]+), the neutral molecule is 179.034.\n\nLet me try C9H5NO3:\n9(12.000) + 5(1.00783) + 14.00307 + 3(15.99491) = 108 + 5.03915 + 14.00307 + 47.98473 = 175.02695 (too low)\n\nC10H9O3:\n10(12.000) + 9(1.00783) + 3(15.99491) = 120 + 9.07047 + 47.98473 = 177.0552\nAdding H+: 178.063 (too low)\n\nC6H9NO5 with [M+H]+ = 176.056 (no)\n\nLet me try C9H9NO3 = 179.058, [M+H]+ = 180.066 (slightly high)\n\nActually, let me try C6H5NO5:\n6(12.000) + 5(1.00783) + 14.00307 + 5(15.99491) = 72 + 5.03915 + 14.00307 + 79.97455 = 171.01677\n[M+H]+ = 172.025 (too low)\n\nC10H5NO2:\n10(12.000) + 5(1.00783) + 14.00307 + 2(15.99491) = 120 + 5.03915 + 14.00307 + 31.98982 = 171.03204\n[M+H]+ = 172.040 (too low)\n\nLet me try C9H9NO3 which gives 180.066 for [M+H]+. The difference from 180.042 is 0.024, which might be within acceptable range or I need to consider other formulas.\n\nActually, C6H9NO5 gives exact mass 175.048, so [M+H]+ would be 176.056, not matching.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe it's [M+Na]+ instead of [M+H]+?\nIf m/z 180.042 is [M+Na]+, then M = 180.042 - 22.990 = 157.052\n\nC7H9NO3 = 7(12) + 9(1.00783) + 14.00307 + 3(15.99491) = 84 + 9.07047 + 14.00307 + 47.98473 = 155.05827 (close!)\n\nActually, let me try C6H9NO4:\n6(12) + 9(1.00783) + 14.00307 + 4(15.99491) = 72 + 9.07047 + 14.00307 + 63.97964 = 159.05318\n[M+Na]+ = 182.043 (too high)\n\nC7H9NO3 = 155.058, [M+Na]+ = 178.048 (too low)\n\nLet me go back to [M+H]+ = 180.042, so M = 179.034\n\nC10H5NO2 = 171.032, [M+H]+ = 172.040 (no)\n\nHmm, let me try C6H5NO5:\nExact: 6(12.000) + 5(1.00783) + 14.00307 + 5(15.99491) = 171.017\n[M+H]+ = 172.025 (no)\n\nLet me try with different elements. What if there's a halogen?\n\nC9H9NO3 seems most reasonable. The formula would be C9H9NO3.\n\nWait, let me recalculate C9H9NO3 more carefully with exact masses:\nC: 12.0000\nH: 1.007825\nN: 14.003074\nO: 15.994915\n\n9(12.0000) + 9(1.007825) + 1(14.003074) + 3(15.994915)\n= 108.0000 + 9.070425 + 14.003074 + 47.984745\n= 179.058244\n\n[M+H]+ = 179.058244 + 1.007825 = 180.066069